RELOAD documentation, last updated on 10 Sep 2026

* Overview.

RELOAD installs a new ircd binary and puts it into service without
disconnecting the server's registered local clients.  It is meant for
routine ircd upgrades: replace the binary at the configured SPATH, then
ask the running server to re-exec itself.  The same code path is also
reachable by sending the running process SIGUSR2.

A reload is not free of disruption.  Server links are always dropped and
have to reconnect and re-burst -- expect a brief netsplit on every
occasion, and the network will see this server part and rejoin.  Channel
timestamps are carried across the reload, so the re-burst does not
net-ride any channel: no deopping, no ban wipe.  A *client* disconnect is
not expected, and is the one thing this feature exists to avoid; the
exceptions are listed under "What is dropped", below.

* Command.

  RELOAD
  RELOAD DUMP <name>

Plain RELOAD requires the RESTART privilege.  It logs the request, then
attempts the sequence described below.  If the sequence aborts, the
command returns to the caller normally; if it succeeds, the process
execs and the command never returns -- the connection that issued it is
one of the ones carried across, unless it happens to be a TLS session
that could not be offloaded to the kernel, in which case typing RELOAD
closes that oper's own connection along with every other non-carriable
one.

RELOAD DUMP is documented in its own section, below.

* The reload sequence.

1. Cheap sanity checks.  The configured server binary (SPATH) must be
   executable, and a temporary file must be creatable.  Either failure
   aborts immediately -- this is what a half finished install or a full
   or read-only temp directory looks like.

2. The server writes a full dump of its state to the temporary file (see
   "What is carried", below), then forks a pre-flight child: the same
   binary, executed with internal flags that tell it to read the dump,
   rebuild every client and channel in memory, and exit -- without ever
   opening a listening socket or writing to a client.  This is a dry run
   of the new binary against the exact state the real reload would hand
   it.

3. The server waits for the pre-flight child to finish, for up to
   RELOAD_TIMEOUT seconds (default 15; the server clamps this feature to
   the 1-30 range, because the wait blocks the event loop and nothing
   else is served while it runs).

   If the pre-flight child fails -- it does not exist inside the
   deadline, it is killed by a signal, or it exits with a non-zero
   status -- the reload is aborted right here.  Nothing has been shed:
   every server link, every client, every listener is exactly as it was
   before RELOAD was typed.  The reason is logged and sent to opers via
   server notice, e.g. "Reload aborted: pre-flight check failed (exit
   status 1)".  This is the server's insurance against execing into a
   binary that cannot actually serve its own state -- a bad install, an
   incompatible dump format, a typo'd SPATH.

4. Only once the pre-flight has said yes does the server shed anything.
   Server links are SQUIT.  Connections that are not yet registered
   users -- still in the connect/handshake phase, including WEBIRC and
   WebSocket handshakes -- are closed.  TLS sessions whose record layer
   is still living in the TLS library rather than in the kernel are
   closed too, with a notice asking the client to reconnect.  Any LIST
   in progress on a surviving client is cancelled.

5. A second dump is written over the same file, this time reflecting the
   state as it actually is after the shed (send queues now hold the
   QUIT/ERROR lines just queued, closed connections are gone).  A
   failure at this point is unrecoverable -- there is no way back once
   the links are down -- so the server exits outright rather than exec
   into a truncated dump; a process supervisor restarting it is a clean
   cold boot that every remaining client sees as a reconnect.

6. Every file descriptor the new image will need -- the surviving client
   sockets, the listening sockets, and the dump itself -- is kept; every
   other descriptor (the event engine, log files, the resolver socket,
   any iauth child's pipes, the pid file lock) is closed, so nothing
   leaks across the exec.

7. The server execv()s the same binary at the same path, with the same
   process id, passing the descriptor number of the dump on the command
   line.  The new image reads the dump before it touches the
   configuration file, reclaims the inherited listening sockets by
   address and port instead of re-binding them (so there is no window
   where new connections are refused), re-reads ircd.conf exactly as a
   REHASH would, and then rebuilds every carried client and channel from
   the dump.  Each client is re-run through the same authorization check
   REHASH performs (matching Client/I-line blocks); one that no longer
   passes is disconnected with "No longer authorized".  An already-opered
   client keeps the operator privileges recorded in the dump rather than
   having them re-derived from the current Operator blocks -- the same
   behaviour REHASH already has for existing opers.

Because the process id, the descriptor table, and the client and
listener sockets never change, a supervisor watching the pid (systemd,
docker's own pid-1 handling) sees this as the same, continuously running
service throughout.

Whatever arrives on a client or listener socket during the roughly one
second the handoff takes simply waits in the kernel's socket receive
buffer; nothing is lost or read twice.

* What is carried across a reload.

  - Every registered local user: nick, user modes, snomask, channels and
    per-channel status (op, voice, delayed-join), bans they have set,
    topics, pending invites, silence list, away message, negotiated
    IRCv3 capabilities, account name/id/flags, and operator status and
    privileges.
  - G-lines, jupes, S-lines, and netconf entries.
  - The network time offset.
  - Server statistics (connection counts, message and byte counters,
    and the rest of the numbers /STATS reports).
  - Each client's unsent output (sendQ) and unprocessed input (recvQ),
    so nothing queued at the moment of reload is dropped.

* What is dropped, and why.

  - Server links.  A server connection cannot be hot-reloaded; it is
    SQUIT and must reconnect and re-burst on its own.  Because channel
    creation timestamps are part of the dump, the re-burst does not
    net-ride channels back to a pre-op state.

  - Unregistered and handshaking connections: anything that has not yet
    become a registered user, including connections mid-WEBIRC or
    mid-WebSocket handshake.  There is no user state yet worth carrying,
    and the new image has no way to resume a handshake it did not start.

  - TLS sessions whose record layer is not kernel-offloaded at the
    moment of reload.  The library object that holds the session's keys
    and record state lives in this process' memory, not in the kernel,
    and it does not survive the exec.  Such a client gets a clean TLS
    close and must reconnect.  See "Kernel TLS requirements", below, for
    how to make a session offloadable so it survives instead.

  - A LIST in progress is ended; the client must issue LIST again
    afterwards.

  - Whowas history, IPcheck's clone/throttle history, and the rolling
    userload averages.  These are caches and statistics windows, not
    user-visible state, and are rebuilt/restarted fresh.

  - Anything in flight that is neither a client nor a server socket:
    outstanding DNS lookups, ident lookups, iauth exchanges, SASL
    exchanges in progress, and UPING requests.  These belong to
    connections that are themselves not carried (they are pre-registration
    activity) or to helper processes that die with the old image.

* Kernel TLS requirements.

A TLS client's session survives a reload only when its record layer has
been handed off to the kernel ("kernel TLS", kTLS) before the reload
runs -- otherwise the session state lives in the TLS library and is lost
with the old process image.  What is needed for that hand-off to happen
depends on platform and TLS backend:

  - Linux: the "tls" kernel module must be loaded ("sudo modprobe tls"),
    and either OpenSSL 3.x built with kTLS support, or GnuTLS with
    "[global] ktls = true" set in the system-wide GnuTLS configuration
    file (GnuTLS has no per-session switch for this -- it is a
    library-wide opt-in, so ircu's TLS_KTLS feature has no effect on a
    GnuTLS build).

  - FreeBSD: the kern.ipc.tls.enable sysctl must be set to 1.

  - OpenBSD's libtls has no kernel TLS offload at all.  A server built
    against libtls always sheds its TLS clients on reload; they simply
    reconnect.

Only sessions negotiated with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305 on TLS 1.2 or
TLS 1.3 can be offloaded; anything else stays in the library and is
dropped on reload regardless of platform.

The TLS_KTLS feature (boolean, default TRUE) controls whether this
server's OpenSSL backend asks for kTLS offload at all; turn it off and
no session on this server is ever eligible to survive a reload. There is
one reason to do that: on a kernel without TLS-transmit rekey support
(Linux older than 6.14), a peer-initiated TLS 1.3 KeyUpdate on an
offloaded connection cannot be serviced by the kernel and ends the
connection outright. An operator whose clients send KeyUpdate and who
cannot yet run a kernel with rekey support should set TLS_KTLS to FALSE
and accept that every TLS client reconnects on every reload, rather than
risk that same disconnect landing on any TLS 1.3 KeyUpdate at an
arbitrary moment.

* RELOAD DUMP: writing a state dump to disk.

  RELOAD DUMP <name>

This writes the same state dump a reload would use to a file on disk,
for inspection or debugging, without actually reloading. It is not a
lesser command than RELOAD: the file it produces holds enough to
reconstruct the server's entire client state, so the requirements
around it are correspondingly strict.

  - The caller needs both the RESTART privilege and the DIE privilege.
    A dump is at least as sensitive as the ability to shut the server
    down, so both are required together.
  - <name> must be a plain file name: it must not contain a '/', and it
    must not be "." or "..".  This is enforced so the command can never
    be used to write outside the server's working directory.
  - The file is written into the server's own working directory (DPATH),
    which the daemon chdir()s into at startup.  The <name> given on the
    command is opened relative to that directory; the dump always lands
    there and the location is not an oper-chosen path.
  - The file is opened O_EXCL and O_NOFOLLOW, mode 0600: it is refused
    outright if a file of that name already exists, or if that name is a
    symlink.  A dump never overwrites anything.
  - Every RELOAD DUMP request is logged and announced to opers via
    server notice, whether or not it actually succeeds at writing
    anything.

WARNING: a state dump is written in cleartext and contains sensitive
data -- every local user's real IP address, account name, and operator
privileges; every client's queued but unsent message bytes; and every
channel's key, upass and apass. Treat a dump file exactly as you would
treat the server's own configuration secrets, and delete it once you are
done with it. This is also why the server's working directory (DPATH)
should be somewhere only the server administrator can read.

* Internal flags: -R and -K.

The command line the new image is exec'd with carries "-R <fd>" (and,
for the pre-flight child only, "-K") to tell it that it is starting from
a hot-reload state dump rather than a cold boot. These flags are set by
the reload machinery itself; there is no reason to pass them by hand,
and doing so on a real boot will make the server try to adopt file
descriptors that do not mean what it expects.

* Wire ABI stability.

Most of the dump names things symbolically -- channel modes as their
letters, capabilities and privileges by name, user modes as mode
characters -- so those are self-describing and survive any renumbering.
A handful of fields, though, are stored as their raw bit or enum values:
the client flag bitset (enum Flag), a client's snomask (SNO_* bits), a
G-line's flags (GLINE_*) and local state (enum GlineLocalState), an
S-line's message type and status flags (the SLINE_* bits behind
sl_msgtype_t / sl_flagtype_t), and a channel ban's flags (BAN_*).  For
these, the number written by the old binary is read straight back by the
new one, so the numeric value of every such bit or enumerator MUST stay
identical across an upgrade.  Renumber a bit or reorder an enum and an
old dump silently decodes into the wrong flags on the new image -- no
error, no crash, just corruption.

To keep that from happening by accident, CI guards these values.
ircd/test/hotreload_abi_gen.c prints the compiled value of every guarded
symbol; ircd/test/hotreload_abi.golden is the committed snapshot of that
output; and tools/check-hotreload-abi.sh (run by the build workflow)
fails the build if any guarded symbol loses its value or disappears.
Adding a new flag at the end is allowed -- the check is append-safe and
only reminds you to refresh the golden file.

If you genuinely need to change one of these values, treat it as a dump
format change: bump the dump version in ircd/hotreload_dump.c, add loader
compatibility handling in ircd/hotreload_load.c so an old dump still
decodes correctly, then regenerate the snapshot with
`tools/check-hotreload-abi.sh --update` and commit it alongside the
change.

* Performing an upgrade.

To upgrade a running server in place:

  1. Build or install the new ircd binary at the path this server was
     configured with (SPATH).
  2. Issue /RELOAD as an operator with the RESTART privilege, or send
     the running process SIGUSR2.

The server will run the sequence above against the new binary. If the
new binary cannot serve the current state -- an incompatible dump
format, a build that is missing something the old process's clients
depend on -- the pre-flight step catches it and the reload aborts with
the old binary still running, untouched. If it succeeds, the upgrade is
live within about a second, with every registered client's connection
intact.
